 driving her  child to school and this mother was  seven months pregnant and the police  stopped her from going about ten miles  over the speed limit and she did not  want to get out of the car and she did  not want him to sign the ticket so you  know what they did they tased her not  once not twice but three times and she  turned around and sued and this time the  Ninth Circuit said if we look at all the  circumstances this really wasn't a  serious crime she was committing she  wasn't a threat to the police officers  they didn't have to use this type of  force this is what we call a  constitutional violation because tasers  are relatively recently adopted these  are some of the first cases that we're  seeing that address the circumstance of  whether it's proper to for police to  apply the Tasers to people in low-risk  situations I think it was time for the  court to send a clear message you can't  just use a Taser when you don't like the  suspect you think they're not being  cooperative or even if you're kind of  afraid of them you have to use them only  when there aren't other options and  there's an imminent threat  the Kephart case is not actually that  atypical for these taser cases when you  start to read them you see how often the  Taser is actually used for traffic  violations these are misdemeanor  offenses and being tased is a much more  serious impact than you would expect  when somebody's just speeding or even  going through a stop sign I think that  the Ninth Circuit's decision will  definitely have an impact on the Kephart  lawsuit now what the impact is going to  be is that all judges are now going to  give a closer look at whether it was  unreasonable conduct but one of the  issues in the Kephart case is that the  actual actions by the officers took  place before the decision in the Ninth  Circuit case and so those officers may  be able to claim like the prior officers  did the law wasn't well established the  San Bernardino County Sheriff's  Departm